[debug] [2026-04-04T01:20:44.923Z] <<< [apiv2][status] POST https://cloudaicompanion.googleapis.com/v1beta/projects/kmllk-7f416/locations/global/instances/default:completeTask 200
[debug] [2026-04-04T01:20:44.924Z] <<< [apiv2][body] POST https://cloudaicompanion.googleapis.com/v1beta/projects/kmllk-7f416/locations/global/instances/default:completeTask {"output":{"messages":[{"content":"I can definitely help you with that! Creating operations with proper authentication is crucial for any secure application. Based on the provided schema, here are 4 operations: a query to list all subjects, a query to retrieve a specific user's content, a mutation to create a new session, and a mutation to record content access.\n\nLet's break them down:\n\n1.  **List All Subjects (Query):** This operation will allow any user (even unauthenticated ones) to see the available subjects. This is a common requirement for the public-facing part of an app.\n\n    ```graphql\n    query ListAllSubjects @auth(level: PUBLIC, insecureReason: \"This operation is safe to expose to the public\") {\n      subjects {\n        id\n        name\n        description\n      }\n    }\n    ```\n\n2.  **Get My Content (Query):** This operation retrieves all content uploaded by the currently authenticated user. It utilizes `auth.uid` to filter the content, ensuring users can only view their own uploads.\n\n    ```graphql\n    query GetMyContent @auth(level: USER) {\n      contents(where: {tutor: {id: {eq_expr: \"auth.uid\"}}}) {\n        id\n        title\n        contentType\n        fileUrl\n        createdAt\n        subject {\n          name\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    ```\n\n3.  **Create New Session (Mutation):** This mutation allows an authenticated user to schedule a new session. It requires the student, tutor, subject, and session details.\n\n    ```graphql\n    mutation CreateNewSession($studentId: UUID!, $tutorId: UUID!, $subjectId: UUID!, $sessionDate: Date!, $startTime: String!, $endTime: String!, $status: String!) @auth(level: USER) {\n      session_insert(data: {\n        studentId: $studentId,\n        tutorId: $tutorId,\n        subjectId: $subjectId,\n        sessionDate: $sessionDate,\n        startTime: $startTime,\n        endTime: $endTime,\n        status: $status\n      })\n    }\n    ```\n\n4.  **Record Content Access (Mutation):** This mutation records when a student accesses a piece of content. The `studentId_expr: \"auth.uid\"` ensures that the access is attributed to the currently logged-in user, promoting data integrity.\n\n    ```graphql\n    mutation RecordContentAccess($contentId: UUID!) @auth(level: USER) {\n      contentAccess_insert(data: {\n        studentId_expr: \"auth.uid\",\n        contentId: $contentId,\n        accessedAt_expr: \"request.time\"\n      })\n    }\n    ```\n\nThese examples should give you a good starting point for building out your app's GraphQL API with Firebase Data Connect.\n\nHere are a few follow-up questions you might want to consider:\n\n*   How would I add more complex filtering or ordering to the `ListAllSubjects` query?\n*   Can I combine fetching content with its associated reviews or sessions in a single query?\n*   What are the best practices for handling errors and displaying user-friendly messages for these operations in my client application?","author":"model"}]},"outputDataContext":{"additionalContext":{"@type":"type.googleapis.com/google.cloud.cloudaicompanion.v1main.ChatOutputDataContext","relatedContent":{}}},"attributionContext":{"citationMetadata":{}}}
